Here's exactly how the deadlock happens — notice how each transaction locks rows in a different order:

```sql
-- Transaction 1: checkout for order with products [A, B]
BEGIN;
UPDATE inventory SET quantity = quantity - 1 WHERE product_id = 'A';
-- ↑ Acquires exclusive row lock on product A
-- ↓ Now needs lock on B, but Tx2 already holds it → BLOCKS
UPDATE inventory SET quantity = quantity - 1 WHERE product_id = 'B';
COMMIT;

-- Transaction 2 (concurrent): checkout for products [B, A]
BEGIN;
UPDATE inventory SET quantity = quantity - 1 WHERE product_id = 'B';
-- ↑ Acquires exclusive row lock on product B
-- ↓ Now needs lock on A, but Tx1 already holds it → BLOCKS
UPDATE inventory SET quantity = quantity - 1 WHERE product_id = 'A';
COMMIT;

-- RESULT: Circular wait detected after deadlock_timeout (1s)
-- PostgreSQL picks the cheaper transaction and aborts it:
-- ERROR: deadlock detected
-- DETAIL: Process 12345 waits for ShareLock on transaction 67890;
--         blocked by process 67891.
-- Your application MUST catch this and retry the transaction.
```
